@@ -83,6 +83,61 @@ Full mode connects ChatGPT's tool calls back to the current Codex task through t
 [OpenAI tunnel-client](https://github.com/openai/tunnel-client). The tunnel is outbound: it does
 not expose a public IP, open an inbound port, or require router forwarding.
 
+### Canonical tool gateway
+
+The bridge uses one canonical tool registry for both discovery and invocation. Every direct,
+namespaced, custom/freeform, deferred `tool_search`, and code-mode tool is normalized to an exact
+wire identity before it reaches the turn broker. The same descriptor is then carried through the
+tool lifecycle, so namespace, native kind, schema, output schema, annotations, execution metadata,
+protocol type, and provenance cannot be reconstructed differently on separate paths.
+
+```text
+Codex / ChatGPT Web request
+        |
+        v
+Adapter + Responses parser
+        |
+        v
+Canonical Tool Registry
+- exact wireName identity
+- immutable descriptor
+- duplicate identities fail closed
+        |
+        v
+Tool Gateway
+- discovery and invocation use the same resolver
+        |
+        v
+Turn Broker
+- immutable request snapshot
+- descriptor / wire / kind consistency checks
+- same-call-ID at-least-once redelivery
+        |
+        v
+Native Codex execution policy
+- approvals
+- sandboxing
+- permissions
+- native retry semantics
+        |
+        v
+Exact tool/provider -> canonical result -> JSON/SSE response
+```
+
+MCP annotations remain descriptive metadata; the bridge does not reinterpret them as authorization
+policy. Unknown or inconsistent tool identities fail closed instead of silently falling back to a
+different tool. The broker deliberately does not add a generic retry engine, universal resource
+locks, a durable journal, or provider idempotency semantics that the native Codex runtime does not
+guarantee.
+
+Tool selection is capability-first. Relevant Codex skills remain workflow guidance, while the Full
+harness directs the Web model to prefer the most specific purpose-built domain, MCP, app, or
+connector tool whose declared schema matches the requested operation. Generic shell, raw HTTP,
+browser automation, and code execution remain available as fallbacks when no higher-level tool can
+perform the operation. Tool discovery is used for configured integrations that are not already
+declared directly in the current turn, so adding future MCP/app tools does not require hard-coding
+their wire names into the bridge.
+
 The launcher's **MCP** page guides the complete setup. For the exact clicks, see the
 [video walkthroughs](TROUBLESHOOTING.md).
 
